    "file": "/repo/app/lib/corpus/opfsAdapter.ts",
    "line": 58,
    "summary": "Path-normalization strip logic `path.replace(/^\\/+|\\/+$/g, \"\").split(\"/\").filter(Boolean)` is written out three times within this same diff: opfsAdapter.ts splitPath (line 58), opfsAdapter.ts readdir (line 128), and the test fake's normalize() in __tests__/inMemoryCorpusFs.ts (line 14). paths.ts documents itself as 'the only source of corpus paths' and is the natural home for one exported helper (e.g. `normalizeCorpusPath(path): string[]` / `splitCorpusPath`), which all three call sites should use.",
    "failure_scenario": "The fake and the real OPFS adapter each own a private copy of the normalization rule, so a future tweak (e.g. also collapsing '//' interior runs, or rejecting '.'/'..' segments) applied to splitPath but not to inMemoryCorpusFs.normalize makes the two substrates disagree on which key a path maps to. The shared contract suite (corpusFsContract.ts) then passes against a fake whose semantics no longer match production OPFS — exactly the substitutability guarantee the contract tests exist to verify. Same risk between splitPath and readdir inside the adapter itself: readdir('a/b/') and writeFile('a/b/x') could drift to different directory resolutions."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/corpus/storeAdapter.ts",
    "line": 25,
    "summary": "createDebouncedLocalStorage() was extracted verbatim from workspaceStore.ts into this new shared module, but an identical debounced-localStorage adapter still exists as a private copy in /repo/app/lib/stores/evidenceStore.ts (createDebouncedStorage, lines 20-41), whose comment even says 'same pattern as workspaceStore'. Now that the diff created an exported shared version, evidenceStore.ts should import createDebouncedLocalStorage from storeAdapter.ts (or a neutral shared location) instead of keeping its own copy.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two byte-identical 300ms setTimeout/clearTimeout debounce implementations (differing only in the console.warn string) now live in the codebase, one exported and one private. A fix to the shared one — e.g. flushing the pending write on pagehide so the last 300ms of edits aren't lost on tab close, or changing the debounce interval — silently does not apply to the evidence store, giving the two persisted stores different data-loss windows. It also means the evidence store never gets the CorpusFS seam when S4 migrates persistence."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/lib/corpus/paths.ts",
    "line": 36,
    "summary": "workspaceSlug()/safeSegment() are the codebase's third independent name-sanitizer: /repo/app/lib/utils/export.ts:34 already exports sanitizeFilename() (strip unsafe chars, collapse whitespace to hyphens, lowercase, 'untitled' fallback) and /repo/app/lib/utils/latexParser.ts:121 has a private slugify(). The corpus versions add real requirements (NFKD, throw-on-empty traversal guard), so they can't just call sanitizeFilename as-is — but the three rule sets should be consolidated into one shared slug utility (e.g. lib/utils/slug.ts) that export.ts and paths.ts both build on, rather than a third parallel regex.",
    "failure_scenario": "The same workspace title now produces different names depending on code path: sanitizeFilename('Théorème #1') keeps spaces-as-hyphens but drops accented chars entirely ('thorme-1'-style), while workspaceSlug NFKD-decomposes to 'theoreme-1'. An exported download and the on-disk corpus folder for the same workspace get unrelated names, and any future 'export the corpus folder' feature has to reconcile two drifting sanitization rule sets. Each future edge-case fix (length caps, reserved Windows names like 'CON') must be remembered in three places."
  },
