    "file": "/repo/app/lib/corpus/manifest.ts",
    "line": 60,
    "summary": "Local `function isObject(v): v is Record<string, unknown>` is a third identical copy of the type guard already defined in /repo/app/lib/utils/workspacePersistence.ts:116 and /repo/app/lib/stores/workspaceStore.ts:32. Export it once (workspacePersistence.ts is the natural owner given manifest.ts sits beside it in the persistence domain, or a tiny lib/utils/guards.ts) and import it here.",
    "failure_scenario": "Minor but compounding: three copies of the same guard means a semantic fix (e.g. deciding arrays should count as objects for some validation, or narrowing to exclude class instances) lands in one copy and not the others, and each new persistence/validation module keeps cargo-culting its own. The diff was an opportunity to converge on one exported guard and instead added the third copy."
  }
]
```

Checks that came back clean (no finding): `flag.ts` — no existing feature-flag helper exists in the codebase to reuse (`isCorpusEnabled` is the first); `CorpusError` — the only existing error class is `OpenRouterError` in `app/lib/llm/callLlm.ts`, a different domain; TextEncoder/TextDecoder — no existing shared wrapper exists (each of `streamLlm.ts`, `transformSseStream.ts`, `formalization/api.ts` instantiates its own), so `manifest.ts`/`storeAdapter.ts` doing likewise matches house style; `parseManifest` vs `loadWorkspace` in `workspacePersistence.ts` — superficially similar JSON-parse-and-validate, but intentionally opposite failure semantics (fail-loud vs coerce-to-defaults, documented in the manifest header), so not duplication; `throttle.ts` is trailing-edge throttle, not a debounce, so the debounced storage couldn't have reused it directly.

